WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.810
A game greets you by name,

2
00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:04.930
and knows your score.

3
00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:07.830
How does it remember?

4
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.920
It keeps each thing in a variable,

5
00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.760
a name with a value inside.

6
00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:18.470
Today: variables, and programs

7
00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:19.770
that ask you questions.

8
00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:24.250
A variable is a name that holds a value.

9
00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:27.320
The equals sign puts the value on the

10
00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.220
right into the name on the left.

11
00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.020
After that, wherever you write the

12
00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:34.280
name, Python uses its value.

13
00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:36.950
A variable can change.

14
00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:41.430
Score equals score plus ten takes the old

15
00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:44.630
score, adds ten, and stores the new score.

16
00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:47.770
The old value is gone: a variable

17
00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:49.570
holds one value at a time.

18
00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:52.370
To ask the person using your

19
00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:54.770
program a question, use input.

20
00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.920
The program waits until they type

21
00:00:57.920 --> 00:00:59.300
an answer and press Enter.

22
00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:01.170
Store the answer in a

23
00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:02.610
variable, and use it.

24
00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:04.800
Here’s the trap.

25
00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:07.720
input always gives back text,

26
00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.260
even if you type a number.

27
00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:13.960
Text plus text sticks together: five plus

28
00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:16.620
three in quotes gives fifty-three!

29
00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:18.840
Turn the answer into a number

30
00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:20.560
with int, and the sum works.

31
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:22.300
Quick check!

32
00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:24.540
Score is twenty.

33
00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:27.770
Then score equals score plus five.

34
00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:29.880
What does print score show?

35
00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:31.650
Twenty-five.

36
00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:34.830
In Python, the equals sign doesn’t

37
00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:35.930
mean the same as in maths.

38
00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:39.310
It means: work out the right side, then

39
00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:41.430
store it in the name on the left.

40
00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:44.750
Now your programs can talk back.

41
00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.300
Ask for a name and say hello.

42
00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:50.810
Ask for an age and say how old

43
00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:52.130
they’ll be in ten years.

44
00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:54.990
Ask for phone credit and a price,

45
00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:56.710
and say if there’s enough.

46
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.180
Choose clear names: score, age, price.

47
00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:05.580
Names can’t have spaces, so use an

48
00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:08.720
underscore, like phone underscore credit.

49
00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:12.340
Capital letters count: Score and

50
00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:14.320
score are two different names.

51
00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:18.150
In the app, open Bug hunt and

52
00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:19.630
try bugs five and six.

53
00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:22.700
In one, a name is spelt wrong.

54
00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:25.200
In the other, text and a

55
00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:26.360
number are added together.

56
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.840
Read the error, fix it and run it again.

57
00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:31.750
Your turn.

58
00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:35.190
A program asks your age with

59
00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:38.930
input, then prints age plus one.

60
00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:41.280
You type thirteen.

61
00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:42.850
What happens?

62
00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:44.870
An error.

63
00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:47.940
Remember: a variable is a name

64
00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:49.760
with one value at a time.

65
00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:51.930
The equals sign stores.

66
00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:55.000
input asks a question and

67
00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:56.400
always gives text.

68
00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.260
Use int to turn it into a number.

69
00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:03.040
Try it yourself: write a program that

70
00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:05.680
asks for your name and your age, then

71
00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:07.680
says how old you’ll be in ten years.

72
00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:10.440
Try it on a friend or someone at home!
